  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a 1-methylpiperidin-4-yl ester of 2,2-diphenyl-2-propoxyacetic acid, i.e. a propiverine-like tertiary-amine antimuscarinic ester. The closest human-context analogs in the visible records, solifenacin and meperidine, have measured absolute oral bioavailability well above 20%: solifenacin 88% (95% CI 75.8-102.1%) after 10 mg oral versus IV, and meperidine normal F ~48% with cirrhosis values 0.80-0.87.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C04","C19","C38","C39","C58","C83","C87","C18","C37","C43","C74"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 intestinal absorption/permeability records for structurally related tertiary-amine antimuscarinics and highly absorbed drugs support a favorable absorption component: biperiden is high-permeability BCS class I, trihexyphenidyl has Caco-2 Papp 46.29 +/- 8.31 x10^-6 cm/s with ER 0.22, cetirizine has high intestinal absorption, levocetirizine has fraction absorbed >=85%, scopolamine has fraction absorbed 100%, phenylbutazone is completely absorbed from the GI tract, and aspirin is highly permeable with human fraction absorbed 87 +/- 12%.",
      "card_ids": ["C146","C160","C187","C170","C182","C147","C171","C148","C156","C185"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall efflux records for related basic amines show high absorptive permeability and no meaningful efflux: trihexyphenidyl ER 0.22, SUVN-G3031 ER 0.81 and not a P-gp substrate, compound 5v ER 1.0, piperazide ER 0.91, minzasolmin ER <=2, compound 2 ER 0.75, and asimadoline P-gp does not limit net uptake. These support limited gut-wall efflux for the query's structural class.",
      "card_ids": ["C224","C200","C211","C213","C194","C221","C215","C218","C226","C228","C236","C212"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 records showing P-gp-mediated efflux for loperamide, fexofenadine, methadone, fentanyl, and trospium are molecule-specific and not transferable to the query; trospium is a quaternary ammonium antimuscarinic, unlike the query's tertiary amine, and fexofenadine is a zwitterionic P-gp substrate. They do not provide affirmative low-F evidence for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C195","C220","C192","C237","C210","C198","C203"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C224","C200","C211","C213","C194","C221","C215","C218","C226","C228","C236","C212"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule in the supplied records.",
    "No query-specific human PK data under matched formulation, prandial state, or disease conditions.",
    "No query-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism assay; L5 records are for other molecules.",
    "Ester hydrolysis and first-pass metabolism of the query are not quantified in the cards.",
    "No query-specific solubility or dissolution data; fexofenadine solubility records are formulation-specific and not transferable."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because the query's tertiary-amine antimuscarinic ester scaffold aligns with high-F human contexts (solifenacin ~88-90%, meperidine normal ~48%) and L4/L5 intestinal absorption and efflux records for related basic amines are supportive (high permeability, low efflux ratios). No affirmative threshold-relevant low-F evidence exists; L5 P-gp substrate records for unrelated molecules are not transferable. Confidence remains high."
}

